= A Meditation upon a Broomstick / 扫帚沉思录

#html.p(id: "en-H001")[#text("According to the style and manner of the Honourable Robert Boyle’s Meditations.")]

#zh(id: "H001")[#text("仿罗伯特·博伊尔阁下《沉思录》的文风与笔法。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("THIS single stick, which you now behold ingloriously lying in that neglected corner, I once knew in a flourishing state in a forest: it was full of sap, full of leaves, and full of boughs: but now, in vain does the busy art of man pretend to vie with nature, by tying that withered bundle of twigs to its sapless trunk: it is now, at best, but the reverse of what it was, a tree turned upside down, the branches on the earth, and the root in the air; it is now handled by every dirty wench, condemned to do her drudgery, and by a capricious kind of fate, destined to make other things clean, and be nasty itself: at length, worn to the stumps in the service of the maids, it is either thrown out of doors, or condemned to the last use, of kindling a fire. When I beheld this, I sighed, and said within myself, Surely man is a Broomstick! nature sent him into the world strong and lusty, in a thriving condition, wearing his own hair on his head, the proper branches of this reasoning vegetable, until the axe of intemperance has lopped off his green boughs, and left him a withered trunk: he then flies to art, and puts on a periwig, valuing himself upon an unnatural bundle of hairs (all covered with powder) that never grew on his head; but now, should this our broomstick pretend to enter the scene, proud of those birchen spoils it never bore, and all covered with dust, though the sweepings of the finest lady's chamber, we should be apt to ridicule and despise its vanity. Partial judges that we are of our own excellencies, and other men's defaults!")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("眼前这根棍子，如今毫不起眼地躺在那个无人理会的角落。我却曾见过它在森林中枝繁叶茂的模样：满是汁液，满是叶片，满是枝条。如今，人类忙忙碌碌的技艺，徒然想与自然争胜，把那一束枯枝绑在它失去汁液的树干上。它充其量只是自己往昔的倒影：一棵倒立的树，枝条朝地，根部朝天。如今，它任脏兮兮的丫头摆弄，注定替她干粗活；命运偏又反复无常，让它把别的东西弄干净，同时把自己弄脏。最后，在伺候女仆的过程中，它磨损得只剩残梗，要么被扔出门外，要么注定最后再效劳一次，拿来生火。我看见这些，不禁叹息，心里说道：人确实是一把扫帚！自然把他送进世界时，他强健而有活力，生机盎然，头上长着自己的头发——那是这株有理性的植物本该拥有的枝叶。后来，放纵的斧头砍掉他的绿枝，只留下枯萎的树干；他便求助于人工，戴上假发，把一束从未长在自己头上、又扑满粉的不自然毛发，看得格外珍贵。可是，如果我们的扫帚也想登场炫耀，为那些它从未生长过的桦树枝洋洋得意，浑身沾满灰尘——哪怕那是从最漂亮的淑女闺房扫来的——我们一定会嘲笑、鄙视它的虚荣。我们竟如此偏心：评判自己的优点，与评判别人的缺点，用的全是两套标准！")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("But a broomstick, perhaps you will say, is an emblem of a tree standing on its head; and pray what is man, but a topsyturvy creature, his animal faculties perpetually mounted on his rational, his head where his heels should be, grovelling on the earth! and yet, with all his faults, he sets up to be a universal reformer and corrector of abuses, a remover of grievances, rakes into every slut's corner of nature, bringing hidden corruption to the light, and raises a mighty dust where there was none before; sharing deeply all the while in the very same pollutions he pretends to sweep away: his last days are spent in slavery to women, and generally the least deserving; till worn out to the stumps, like his brother besom, he is either kicked out of doors, or made use of to kindle flames for others to warm themselves by.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("不过，也许你会说，扫帚不过是一棵倒立之树的象征。那请问，人又是什么？不也是个上下颠倒的东西吗？他的兽性始终凌驾于理性之上，头待在脚跟本该待的地方，匍匐着贴向地面！然而，尽管缺点这么多，他仍把自己摆成普天下的改革家与弊端纠正者，仿佛专为消除冤屈而来，伸进自然中每个邋遢女人的角落，把隐藏的污秽扫到光下，在原本没有尘土的地方扬起漫天灰尘。与此同时，他自己却深陷在那些声称要扫除的污秽里。他最后的岁月用来给女人当奴仆，往往还是最不值得他效劳的女人；直到像他的扫帚兄弟一样，耗损得只剩残梗，要么被赶出门外，要么被拿去点起火焰，供别人取暖。")]
